WEBVTT

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:13.630
Yet, less than a mile from ground zero, a human being in a shelter should have survived.

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:15.530
That was the end.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:17.480
Here was the beginning.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.580
These, the characters in this drama of survival under the A-bomb,

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:26.490
a program being conducted by Federal Civil Defense, the Atomic Energy Commission,

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:29.970
and the Department of Defense to show the nation the effect.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.860
effects of atomic blasts on homes, home-type shelters, and automobiles.

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:41.370
A variety of passenger cars, old and new, loaned by the dealers and manufacturers of America.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:44.280
The time, March 1953.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:51.460
The scene, Las Vegas, as the caravan sets out for the site of AEC's Nevada Proving Ground.

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:57.970
There, the test will be held in connection with a shot in the Atomic Energy Commission's developmental series.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:07.670
Yucca Flat, a barren area of desert, sagebrush and joshua trees now suddenly comes alive with

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:14.060
activity. On each car, instruments to measure excessive temperature increases. Also a film

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:20.180
badge for detection of gamma radiation. The vehicles, identified by number, are stationed

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:26.530
at various ranges from ground zero. Into each car go these lifelike department store mannequins

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:29.960
donated by private sources to help determine the effects.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.820
of atomic blast. And here, one of the two typical American frame dwellings to be

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:44.030
tested. This one, 7,500 feet from ground zero. Each home is to reveal effects of

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:53.120
atomic blast only. Indoors are no utilities that might cause fire. Venetian

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:58.700
blinds on windows facing the blast help keep out heat flash. They are not

00:01:58.700 --> 00:01:59.860
supposed to resist

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:06.540
blast within the house scenes typical of the american family at home first floor living room

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:17.550
first floor dining room children at play unaware of approaching disaster a sick bed upstairs yes

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:24.740
a family unprepared without the simple box type shelter shown here in a model in such a shelter

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:29.780
a family has prepared and awaits the bomb soon to explode from this 300

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.270
foot tower nearly a mile and a half away. House number one, 3,500 feet from ground zero.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:44.530
1,450 feet away, this outdoor shelter of reinforced concrete. Entrances to other outdoor shelters

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:51.110
to be tested. Here, a model provides an interior view of a basement exit type home shelter.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:58.040
One of these was only 1,250 feet from ground zero. Meanwhile, at Las Vegas,

00:02:58.450 --> 00:02:59.970
newsmen and photographers arrived.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.160
from all over the nation to cover an event that has a direct bearing on the welfare of

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:11.580
every American family. This way to a general briefing by officials of the Atomic Energy

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:17.730
Commission, Defense Department, and Civil Defense. Then out to Yucca Flat. Here, just

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:23.740
before dawn, for the first time in our history, American homes will be exposed to atomic blast.

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:29.280
Arriving are Civil Defense observers, representatives of all the nation's news media.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.930
Today, Main Street of every American city and town.

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:43.600
House number two is inspected as preparations continue for detonation of the bomb at 5.20 next morning, 2 a.m.

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:47.280
Our troops move into trenches two miles from ground zero.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:51.320
With them is Federal Civil Defense Administrator Val Peterson.

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:58.170
In the darkness of News Knob, seven and a half miles from ground zero, nearly a thousand observers gather.

00:03:58.660 --> 00:03:59.960
Civil Defense officials...

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.810
From all over the nation, newsreel, radio and television crew, newsmen and magazine writers,

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:11.180
ready to bring to the families of America this drama of survival under the A-bomb.

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:13.720
The hours slowly tick away.

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:19.200
Then the minutes, 10 seconds to 5.20 a.m.

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:25.880
Five seconds, four, three, two, one, zero.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:34.140
House number one as the blast hits it.

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:40.120
Unused knobs, civil defense observers witness effects of the blast and the tremendous glare of the fireball.

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:49.760
Here again is house number one collapsing.

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:52.050
Show now in stop motion.

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:53.840
First an intense light.

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:58.340
The heat flash strikes, charring the outer surface of the house without causing fire.

00:04:58.910 --> 00:04:59.910
Foaming blackened.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:06.190
particles of whitewash are forced upward by the heat flash in the right corner of the screen smoke

00:05:06.190 --> 00:05:12.980
rises from the wooden base of the metal floodlight pole the scene darkens as the blast wave brings

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:21.440
heavy clouds of dust forward part of the roof is snapped upward like a lid crashing into the rear

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:28.580
yard now the blast wave gets inside and the house under tremendous pressure blows apart remember

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:29.980
what you have seen here

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:37.310
and detail happened in just two and one-third seconds. Within minutes after the blast, our

00:05:37.310 --> 00:05:43.160
troops move into no man's land as part of their own test exercise. Civil defense teams could

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:50.680
have moved in just as quickly to rescue the injured. In the cold light of dawn, television

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:55.980
cameras on news knob make the threat of the mushroom cloud a dark reality in homes across

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:59.980
the nation. First to arrive from the forward area is

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.700
Federal Civil Defense Administrator, Val Peterson.

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:08.120
Escorted to News Knob, the top FCDA official reports his experience.

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:11.840
In the trench with our troops only two miles from ground zero,

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:16.010
he suffered no effects from blast, heat, or radiation.

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:24.320
Information about test results is given by Dr. Alvin C. Graves,

00:06:24.790 --> 00:06:26.890
Atomic Energy Commission Test Director.

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:37.150
radiological safety teams begin checking the forward area to determine the level of radiation

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:45.080
house number two farthest from ground zero badly damaged but still standing on house number two

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:53.470
exterior blown out windows sashes and frames remember this family they did not take shelter

00:06:53.470 --> 00:06:59.750
their living room after the blast mannequins thrown about clothing cut plaster bodies

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:01.380
Marked by flying glass.

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:03.660
Remember this dining room group?

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:08.380
Again, effects of an atomic blast in the house farthest from Ground Zero.

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:18.440
Injury, perhaps death, in a tangle of debris, the result of being unprepared.

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:22.160
Second floor, bedroom, plaster and windows damaged.

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:24.390
Roof sprung, rafters broken.

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:29.960
House number one, nearest Ground Zero, almost complete...

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.960
A mass of rubble and debris, the roof blown into several sections.

00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:41.960
Now you enter house number one, first floor disintegrated, second floor collapsed.

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:46.270
No protection for this family trapped in debris.

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:54.320
Note the pushed back wooden girders, blocked the stairway into the basement.

00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:57.960
Part of the kitchen and dining room have fallen into the basement.

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:08.100
Yet, in this lean-to shelter, you discover indications that a human being might have survived the blast with simple protection.

00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:13.080
In house number two, a cracked beam above the basement box-type shelter.

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:14.910
But within, this...

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:21.360
family which was prepared was neither moved nor harmed. Outdoors at various ranges from

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:27.350
Ground Zero, nearly every car driven into the area could be driven out again under its

00:08:27.350 --> 00:08:29.210
own power in spite of damage.

00:08:31.148 --> 00:08:35.248
Headlines brought the Operation Doorstep story into the homes of 30 million families.

00:08:35.532 --> 00:08:37.772
left them wondering how they might be affected.

00:08:38.316 --> 00:08:40.016
Engineering research goes on.

00:08:40.364 --> 00:08:42.364
Other civil defense tests will be held.

00:08:42.796 --> 00:08:44.536
But already we know these facts.

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:49.520
An outdoor shelter provides the best chance for survival from atomic blast.

00:08:50.060 --> 00:08:50.900
Second safest.

00:08:51.148 --> 00:08:53.708
a basement lean-to or box-type shelter.

00:08:54.316 --> 00:08:57.316
Your local civil defense office can provide construction data

00:08:57.584 --> 00:08:59.924
for these simple, inexpensive home shelters.

00:09:00.892 --> 00:09:01.372
Today.

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:04.120
There is no second best.

00:09:04.508 --> 00:09:06.008
for family's civil defense.

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:10.600
the urgent need to prepare now against the threat of atomic warfare.

00:09:13.372 --> 00:09:14.092
Or will you?

00:09:14.716 --> 00:09:15.516
like a mannequin.

00:09:16.252 --> 00:09:16.832
Just sit.

00:09:17.276 --> 00:09:17.876
And wait.
